## 📈 Visualization

### Trip Volume Heatmap

The primary visualization is a 24x7 heatmap showing trip counts for each hour-weekday combination:

- **X-axis:** Days of the week (Monday through Sunday)
- **Y-axis:** Hours of the day (00:00 - 23:00)
- **Color scale:** 
  - Red: Low demand (minimum 597 trips)
  - White: Average demand (centered at ~6,483 trips)
  - Green: High demand (maximum 12,369 trips at Tuesday 5 PM)
- **Cell values:** Formatted with commas for readability (e.g., "12,369")

**Key patterns visible in the heatmap:**
- Intense green during weekday evenings (5-7 PM)
- Deep red during early morning hours (2-5 AM)
- Gradient shift showing weekend demand extending later into the night
- Friday-Saturday late night activity extension through midnight

![Uber Trips Heatmap](uber_trips_heatmap.png)
*Heatmap showing trip volume patterns across hours and days of the week*

## Key Features

### 1. Data Cleaning & Transformation
- Parsed date/time strings into separate date, time, hour, and weekday components
- Created custom formatting functions for consistent date format (YYYY/MM/DD)
- Implemented hour formatting with leading zeros for proper alphabetical sorting
- Converted strings to datetime objects for temporal operations

### 2. Data Visualization
- 24x7 heatmap showing trip volume by hour and day of week
- Custom diverging color scheme (red-white-green) centered on midpoint
- Comma-separated value formatting for readability
- Chronologically ordered weekdays (Monday-Sunday) for intuitive interpretation

### 3. Statistical Analysis
- Frequency analysis using `.value_counts()` to identify peak periods
- Descriptive statistics (mean, median, standard deviation) for demand variability
- Min/max identification across multiple dimensions (day, hour, minute)
- Pivot table aggregation for cross-tabulation analysis

### 4. Code Quality
- Modular helper functions (`date_format()`, `hour_format()`)
- Comprehensive unit tests using pytest
- Clear documentation and inline comments
- Type hints for function parameters

## 🚀 How to Run

### Prerequisites
```bash
pip install pandas matplotlib seaborn pytest
```

### Running the Analysis
```bash
# Navigate to project directory
cd uber-trips-analysis-folder/uber-trips-analysis

# Run the main analysis (generates heatmap)
python time_file.py

# Run unit tests
pytest test_time_file.py
```

### Dataset
The analysis uses `uber-raw-data-sep14.csv` containing September 2014 Uber trip data with columns:
- `Date/Time` - Timestamp of trip pickup
- `Lat` - Pickup latitude coordinate
- `Lon` - Pickup longitude coordinate  
- `Base` - TLC base company code

**Dataset size:** 1,028,136

## 📁 Project Structure
```
uber-trips-analysis-folder/
├── uber-trips-analysis/
│   ├── time_file.py              # Main analysis script
│   ├── test_time_file.py         # Unit tests for helper functions
│   └── uber-raw-data-sep14.csv   # Dataset (not included in repo)
├── .venv/                         # Virtual environment
└── README.md                      # Project documentation
```
### Key Technical Decisions

**Why vectorized operations over loops?**
- Initial attempt using `.apply()` with lambda functions was extremely slow on 1M rows
- Switched to pandas datetime accessor (`.dt`) for 100x+ speed improvement
- Demonstrates understanding of pandas optimization best practices

**Why custom color scheme?**
- Diverging palette (red-white-green) makes anomalies immediately visible
- Centered on midpoint ensures balanced color distribution
- Green/red associations align with business context (high/low demand)

**Why pivot table structure?**
- Enables efficient cross-dimensional aggregation
- Creates matrix format ideal for heatmap visualization
- Built-in pandas functionality ensures correctness

## 📝 Methodology

### Analysis Pipeline
```
Raw CSV Data (1M+ rows)
    ↓
[1] Data Loading
    • Read CSV with pandas
    • Initial data inspection
    ↓
[2] Feature Engineering
    • Extract date components from Date/Time
    • Create Hour, Time, Date, Weekday columns
    • Format values for proper sorting
    ↓
[3] Frequency Analysis
    • Count trips by various dimensions
    • Identify max/min across categories
    • Calculate statistical summaries
    ↓
[4] Pivot Table Construction
    • Aggregate by Hour × Weekday
    • Create matrix structure for heatmap
    • Reorder for chronological display
    ↓
[5] Visualization
    • Generate diverging color scheme
    • Create annotated heatmap
    • Apply formatting for readability
    ↓
Results & Insights
```

